Right—the **kind would be optional classification data**, not a replacement for `tool_name`, and not something the person importing a trace has to add by hand.

Because this project mirrors OpenTraces exactly in [trace.ts](<~/Documents/github/reasoning_vis_oss_all/agent-trace-vis/src/app/agentic-traces/trace.ts>), I’d put our extra classification in OpenTraces’ existing free-form `metadata` field, rather than adding a field to each `tool_call`. For example:

```json
{
  "steps": [{
    "tool_calls": [{
      "tool_call_id": "call-123",
      "tool_name": "grep",
      "input": {
        "pattern": "TODO",
        "path": "src/"
      }
    }]
  }],
  "metadata": {
    "tool_kinds": {
      "call-123": "search.text"
    }
  }
}
```

`tool_name` is still `"grep"` and `input` is still the original input. `tool_kinds` is just an optional hint for our visualizer: “treat this call as text search.” If a converter can’t confidently classify a call, it can leave it out; the viewer can fall back to today’s name-based behavior.

And to clarify my search-string comment: **the search string is already in `input`**, as `"pattern": "TODO"` here. I didn’t mean adding another field or changing what gets stored. The UI might use that existing value to make a short label like “Grep: TODO,” while still keeping the full original tool name and input available to inspect. If it can’t recognize where the search text is in a particular tool’s input, it should show the original input instead—not guess or discard it.

So: one optional kind annotation for classification; original tool name and arguments remain as they were.

Yes—that’s simpler if every call with the same `tool_name` should have the same kind. For example:

```json
{
  "steps": [{
    "tool_calls": [
      { "tool_call_id": "call-1", "tool_name": "grep", "input": { "pattern": "TODO" } },
      { "tool_call_id": "call-2", "tool_name": "grep", "input": { "pattern": "FIXME" } }
    ]
  }],
  "metadata": {
    "tool_kinds": {
      "grep": "search.text"
    }
  }
}
```

The original calls stay unchanged; the wrapper looks up each call’s `tool_name` in `metadata.tool_kinds`. That avoids repeating `"search.text"` for every grep call.

The tradeoff is that this assumes a tool name always means the same kind within a trace. That’s likely a good default for a simple first version. If we later encounter a tool name used for different kinds of work, we could add a per-call override—but we don’t need that complexity unless real traces call for it.

